+A **head** is a revision that no later revision names as a base. The head count defines the visit's state:
+
+- **One head** — the visit is *settled*, and that head is its **current revision**.
+- **More than one head** — the visit is *diverged*, it has no current revision, and it carries unresolved review work.
+
+Only the creating revision has an empty `baseRevisionIds`; a field revision names exactly one base; a resolution revision names every head it collapses. Because every submission is stored as a whole payload, a visit's value at any point in its history is read directly from a revision rather than reconstructed.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+A submission envelope carries the visit ID, the base revision ID the worker edited from (absent when creating the visit), an idempotency key, the complete payload, and the client capture time. The server authenticates the submitting worker, then validates: field types, the plot reference against the site catalog, and that the named base revision exists and belongs to this visit. A submission naming an unknown base revision, or one belonging to another visit, is rejected with that reason — the server cannot place it in a lineage, and guessing a base would risk discarding the worker's starting state.
+
+The idempotency key is unique within a visit. A replay with the same key and an identical payload returns the original acceptance result. Reuse with a different payload is rejected and the original revision is left unchanged.
+
+Acceptance is never withheld because of divergence. A submission whose base is the current revision extends it and the visit stays settled. A submission whose base is any other revision — a head of another branch, the divergence point, or an older ancestor — is accepted and becomes an additional head or extends an existing branch. Rejecting a stale-based edit would discard a field worker's observations to save a coordinator a comparison, and the comparison is the cheaper of the two.
+
+Retrieval distinguishes the two states explicitly. A settled visit returns its current revision. A diverged visit returns its competing head revisions and the **divergence point** — the nearest common ancestor of those heads, the last state that was unambiguously current — labelled as not current. Callers therefore cannot read a usable current value out of a diverged visit by accident. An unresolved-work listing returns the diverged visits for coordinator triage.
+
+## Divergence and Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves a diverged visit by submitting a **resolution**: the contributing head revision IDs and the payload that should stand. The payload is either one head's payload unchanged — adopting that worker's visit — or a payload the coordinator composes after reading the competing revisions. Both land the same way, as a resolution revision naming every contributing head as a base, which collapses the head set to one and settles the visit.
+
+A resolution retains the coordinator's identity, the contributing revision IDs, and its receipt time. Losing heads remain in the graph with their original payloads and authors; nothing is discarded, and a reader can walk from the current revision back through a resolution to every submission that fed it.
+
+A resolution is rejected if it names fewer than all current heads, or names a revision that is no longer a head. Both happen when a worker's submission arrives while a coordinator is deciding; the coordinator re-reads the visit and resolves against the heads that now exist, rather than settling a visit around a state that has moved. Resolution revisions can themselves diverge: a worker editing from an older base after a resolution creates a new head, and the visit returns to review.
+
+## Public Contract
+
+| Endpoint | Method | Request | Response |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `/visits/{visitId}/revisions` | POST | Submission envelope | Acceptance: revision ID, resulting visit state |
+| `/visits/{visitId}` | GET | — | Current revision, or divergence point plus competing heads |
+| `/visits/{visitId}/revisions` | GET | — | Full revision graph with authors and receipt times |
+| `/visits` | GET | `state=diverged` filter | Visits carrying unresolved review work |
+| `/visits/{visitId}/resolutions` | POST | Contributing head IDs, payload | New current revision |
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its base revision; concurrent edits produce competing heads a coordinator resolves | Field-aware three-way merge combining disjoint field changes automatically | Merge rules cannot see scientific relationships between fields, so automatic combination can silently produce a record no observer submitted. A larger review queue is the accepted cost of never combining edits the server does not understand. |
+| Value of a diverged visit | No current revision; retrieval returns competing heads and the divergence point | Return newest head as provisional current; return the divergence point as current | A provisional value is an arrival-order winner by another name, and downstream callers would consume it. Withholding a current value forces the unresolved state to be handled rather than read past. |
+| Stale-based submissions | Accepted as an additional head | Rejected so the tablet refetches and re-edits | Rejection discards observations already recorded in the field; the tablet cannot always recover them from a worker days later. |
+| Partial resolutions | A resolution must name all current heads | Allow pairwise resolution of two heads at a time | Pairwise resolution lets a visit look settled while a third submission is still outstanding. Naming all heads keeps "one head means settled" true at every point. |
+| Resolution representation | New revision with multiple bases, including when a head is adopted unchanged | Mark losing heads superseded in place and keep the adopted head as current | A single mechanism settles both outcomes, and the resolution's author and time stay distinct from the field submission whose payload it adopted. |
 
